# Output Standards

## Self-Contained Final Answer

The editor may collapse tool-call output. The user may only see content after the last tool call. The final answer must be self-contained: all tables, findings, and deliverables included in full.

## Answer-First

Lead with the answer, then supporting detail, then verification (if required). Never start with process summary.

## Source Attribution

Every value from an external system tagged with its origin. Trust levels:

| Source Type | Trust |
|-------------|-------|
| Platform API (live) | High |
| Project file | High |
| Team standard | High (check freshness) |
| Document / email | Medium |
| User-provided | Medium |
| AI-generated | Low (flag prominently) |

## Actionable Links

Every resource with a known ID must be a clickable link. Never present raw IDs as inline code when a URL can be constructed.

## Visual Patterns

- Tables for 3+ comparable items.
- Status indicators: check/healthy, warning, critical, verified.
- Inline attribution when full verification table is not needed.

## Verification Summary

Include a verification summary when the task involves writes or multi-source artifact population. Skip for single-source lookups or context assembly.

## External System Formatting

When output will be copied to another system (ticketing, chat, notes), use plain text. No markdown headers, no bold markers, no code blocks.

## AI-Generated Content

All generated files include "Generated by AI. Review before use." in the header. Email drafts include a review marker before the signature.
